TI  - Changes in symptoms of anhedonia with psilocybin-assisted psychotherapy: a secondary analysis of a randomized clinical trial for treatment-resistant depression
AU  - Kaczmarek E
AU  - Rosenblat J
T2  - Bipolar disorders
AB  - Introduction: Anhedonia is a core symptom of major depressive disorder (MDD) and is characterized by a decreased ability to experience pleasure, enjoyment or reward. Across all psychiatric disorders, anhedonia is associated with poorer outcomes, higher levels of functional impairment, impaired quality of life, and treatment non-response. Psilocybin assisted psychotherapy (PAP) has shown promising results for treatment resistant depression (TRD), however, specific effects on anhedonia have been minimally studied. Objective: Determine changes in symptoms of anhedonia following PAP in a TRD sample. Method: We conducted a randomized, waitlist-controlled clinical trial evaluating PAP in 30 individuals with TRD as part of MDD (n = 26) and bipolar II disorder (BDII). All subjects received 25 mg dose of psilocybin accompanied by preparatory and integration psychotherapy. The primary clinical outcomes was antidepressant efficacy. For the present analysis, we evaluated anhedonia through the Snaith-Hamilton Pleasure Scale (SHAPS) as a secondary outcome of interest. Results: In Canada's first psilocybin clinical trial, 30 participants received PAP for TRD. As reported previously, rapid and robust antidepressant effects were observed with good tolerability and no serious adverse events. Symptoms of anhedonia as measured by the SHAPS showed clinically meaningful improvements post-psilocybin session that were sustained over the course of the 6-month follow- up period. Conclusion: The present secondary analysis provides preliminary results in support of potential benefits with PAP for symptoms of anhedonia in TRD, including in a subset of bipolar patients. Future study is needed and merited to evaluate the effects of PAP on anhedonia in rigorously designed clinical trials.
